          "case_name": "Native Village of Kwinhagak v. State, Department of Health & Social Services, Office of Children's Services",
          "citation": "542 P.3d 1099 (Alaska 2024)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Alaska",
          "year": 2024,
          "date_decided": "2024-02-09",
          "holding": "OCS need not follow the civil commitment statutes to hospitalize a child in its custody for psychiatric care, but the Alaska Constitution's due process clause required prompt notice to the CINA parties and a hearing as soon as reasonably possible; a 46-day delay was unconstitutional and the order was reversed.",
          "relevance": "Is Alaska's controlling authority on the process due when the agency authorizes emergency psychiatric hospitalization of a child in its custody.",
          "quote": "Due process also required the court to hold a hearing as soon as reasonably possible to determine whether the hospitalization was justified.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9476228/native-village-of-kwinhagak-v-state-of-alaska-dhss-ocs/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 9476228
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Wallis ex rel. Wallis v. Spencer",
          "citation": "202 F.3d 1126 (9th Cir. 2000)",
          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit",
          "year": 1999,
          "date_decided": "1999-09-14",
          "holding": "Summary judgment for the city was reversed: a jury could find the officers lacked reasonable cause to seize the children without a court order, and, absent a risk that physical evidence would dissipate or an urgent medical problem requiring immediate attention, the state must notify parents and obtain judicial approval before subjecting a seized child to an investigatory physical examination.",
          "relevance": "Distinguishes genuine emergency medical care, which the state may authorize, from investigatory examinations, which it may not conduct without parental notice and a judicial order.",
          "quote": "the state is required to notify parents and to obtain judicial approval before children are subjected to investigatory physical examinations",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/6985031/wallis-ex-rel-wallis-v-spencer/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 7079922
        }
      ],
